      "id": "1vpqh7t",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Nature of Light"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Light is a transverse electromagnetic wave that can travel through a vacuum without requiring a material medium.",
        "Reflection is the bouncing back of light when it strikes a boundary surface, following the law where the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection.",
        "Refraction occurs when light transitions between media of different optical densities, causing a change in its speed and path.",
        "Dispersion refers to the splitting of composite white light into its constituent spectrum (VIBGYOR) when passing through a prism.",
        "Optical instruments, such as microscopes and telescopes, utilize specific combinations of convex and concave lenses for magnification."
      ],
      "id": "7z2qwzu"
    },
